; This looks like it declares `Int -> Bool`. It does not: the type is
; `Int`, and `Bool` is a second type in a position that holds one.
;
; `skipParens` used to own the declaration's end position, so whatever
; the type parse did not consume was skipped in silence - which typed
; `h` WRONGLY rather than leaving it untyped. The only report was an
; AX3004 at the call site on line 9, blaming the caller ("expected
; function type, found Int") for a defect in the signature above.
(:: h Int Bool)
(fn (h x) (== x 1))
(:: main Int)
(fn (main) (if (h 1) 0 1))
